Q1. How did APT29 get initial access to Microsoft in 
January 2024?Name the specific control that was 
absent. Explain why a test tenant specifically was 
vulnerable when a production tenant may not have 
been.

Apt29 got initial access to microsoft through low 
and slow password spray attack on microsoft's test 
tenant accounts.

MFA and CA was absent.

A test tenant was vulnerable because MFA wasnt 
implemented due to the fact that microsoft thought 
that since it isnt a critical environment like the 
production tenant that the security shoulnt be taken 
seriously.

Q2.Walk through a Scattered Spider help desk vishing 
attack step by step.What exactly does the attacker 
say? What does the help desk agent do?What technical 
capability does the attacker have after the call 
ends?

Step 1: The attacker tries to log in with a 
compromised employee's username and password and 
when mfa is trigerred the call the help desk telling 
them that they cant complete the mfa requirements 
because the lost their mfa device. Step 2: Helpdesk 
asks them to verify who they are by providing some 
personal info. Since the attacker had already stolen 
those info they provide it to the help desk which in 
turn assign a new mfa device to them. Step 3: They 
try to login again using those same credentials but 
this time satisfying mfa. and access is granted to 
the enterprise cloud account of the employee.

After the call ends attacker now have the capabilty 
to enumarate account, escalate privilege, move 
laterally and exfiltrate.

3.What is MagicWeb? What does it allow that Golden 
SAML does not?Why is it more operationally dangerous 
than Golden SAML? 
MagicWeb is a malware created by 
apt29 and designed mainly to attack adfs by 
overriding its authentication result. It allows an 
attacker to actively manipulate authentication 
result without any signing key. Its more dangerous 
then golden saml because even with hardware backed 
key protection it still persists and it doesnt need 
to steal any key to do what golden saml does.

4.How did Storm-0558 forge tokens? What was the root 
cause at theimplementation level? Why did the forged 
tokens pass validation? Storm-0558 forged valid 
oauth tokens using stolen microsoft consumer signing 
key. Root cause was that microsoft's token 
validation code verified signature of tokens but 
doesnt check which key is being used for a specific 
request and if that key is authorized to access a 
given endpoint. eg if a token signed with a consumer 
signing key is authorized to perform action on an 
enterprise endpoint The forged token passed 
validation because its signature was valid and its 
signing key came from a trusted issuer.

5.What do all four groups share in how they think 
about persistence?Not technique — the underlying 
mental model about what persistence means. They 
understood that the highest form of persistence is 
achieved by abusing trusts and leveraging identities 
instead of deploying malwares.
